WHY THE PATCH
-------------
`solve_dc_current` returns powertrain-sign current (P < 0 on discharge => I < 0),
while `dc_voltage = ocv - R0*I - v_rc1 - v_rc2` and both RC updates assume battery-sign
current (discharge positive). The two conventions meet with no conversion. Measured on
the unpatched source: 60 s at I = -100 A with stock R1, R2, TAU1, TAU2 gives
v_rc1 = -10,000 V, v_rc2 = -0,476 V, Udc = 790,5 V against OCV = 780 V — terminal
voltage above OCV while discharging.

The conversion is made once, immediately after the solver, so `dc_voltage`, both RC
updates and the KT2*I**2 heating term are untouched and become correct by construction.


THE NINE EDITS
--------------
(1) main.py:386 — inserted immediately after `dc_current = solve_dc_current(...)`

    +            # Battery perspective from here on: positive = discharge. See PLANT_ORIGIN.
    +            dc_current = -dc_current

(2) main.py:389,391 — the two saturation guards, comparisons flipped

    -            if q_act <= 0.0 and dc_current < 0.0:
    +            if q_act <= 0.0 and dc_current > 0.0:
                     dc_current = 0.0
    -            if q_act >= Q_MAX and dc_current > 0.0:
    +            if q_act >= Q_MAX and dc_current < 0.0:
                     dc_current = 0.0

(3) main.py:402 — coulomb counting

    -            q_act += dc_current * SAMPLE_TIME
    +            q_act -= dc_current * SAMPLE_TIME

(4) signals.json:165 — `dc_current_a` description

    -      "description": "DC current after temperature derating. Negative = discharge, positive = charge.",
    +      "description": "DC current after temperature derating. Positive = discharge, negative = charge (battery perspective).",

(5) main.py:467 — the inter-tick sleep, divided by the new TIME_SCALE parameter

    -            time.sleep(SAMPLE_TIME)
    +            time.sleep(SAMPLE_TIME / p["TIME_SCALE"])

    Only the wall-clock sleep is divided. The integration step stays SAMPLE_TIME at
    main.py:121 (both ALPHA), 402 (coulomb counting) and 426 (heat), so the published
    series is identical at any speed and the four trace sha256s are unaffected — the
    offline generator never sleeps at all: plant/loader.py:160 replaces main.time with
    a shim whose sleep discards its argument. `p` is the per-tick snapshot taken under
    state_lock at main.py:343, the same read the rest of the loop body uses.
    TIME_SCALE's lexicon min of 1.0 is what makes the divide safe; there is no guard.

(6) parameters.json — TIME_SCALE appended as the 19th descriptor

    +      "name": "TIME_SCALE",  "label": "Simulation Speed",  "datatype": "float",
    +      "unit": null,  "default": 1.0,  "min": 1.0,  "max": 50.0,  "tunable": true

    Reformatted here for width; the descriptor and its description are in the file.
    Tunable because it enters no state update, no filter coefficient and no integral,
    so a mid-run write breaks no continuity — the stated reason Q_MAX_AH and
    SAMPLE_TIME are fixed does not apply to it. max 50.0 is a throughput bound:
    ×50 is 500 msg/s on battery-data. model.version stays 1.1.0 because load_lexicon
    takes `model` wholesale from signals.json (lexicon.py:64), so bumping it here
    alone would leave the merged view and the [STARTUP] log reporting a stale number.


(7) signals.json:88-109 — `reset` appended to the input signals, after `heater_setting`

    +      "name": "reset",  "label": "Reset",  "datatype": "enum",
    +      "unit": null,  "default": 0,  "direction": "input",  "tunable": null,
    +      "enum": [{"value": 0, "label": "Idle"}, {"value": 1, "label": "Reset"}]

    Reformatted here for width; the descriptor and its description are in the file.
    `enum` and not `bool`: it is the datatype the lexicon already uses for a two- or
    three-position control (chiller_setting, heater_setting), `coerce` returns the
    canonical member value for it, and `_startup_value` already has the int branch —
    a bool entry would be the lexicon's first and main.py:66 says so in as many words.

    The four trace sha256s are unaffected. The generator writes `cmd` only through
    `adapter.to_plant` (runner.py:86-91 via loader.py:97 and :148), whose return value
    names the four setpoints and never `reset`, so `cmd["reset"]` holds its startup
    default 0 for the whole run and the branch in (8) is never taken. It reads back
    `adapter.from_plant`, which takes five keys out of the payload, so the extra signal
    in the periodic "applied" echo reaches nothing — the same argument that held when
    (6) added TIME_SCALE to the echoed parameter set.

(8) main.py:346-352 and main.py:58 — act on `reset` once, under the tick's own lock

    +                if setpoints["reset"]:
    +                    cmd["reset"] = 0
    +                    q_act = Q_MAX / 2.0
    +                    v_rc1 = v_rc2 = 0.0
    +                    a_thermal_prev = p["A_THERMAL"]
    +                    heat = INITIAL_TEMPERATURE_C / a_thermal_prev
    +                    temperature = a_thermal_prev * heat

    Appended to the `with state_lock` block that already takes `setpoints` and `p`, so
    the flag is read, acted on and cleared without a second acquisition. Clearing `cmd`
    rather than `setpoints` is what makes it momentary: the write lands once, the next
    tick sees 0. The restored set is exactly the state the initialisation block binds —
    `last_echo` is echo scheduling, not model state, and stays. Setpoints and parameters
    are untouched by design: pedals, ambient, heater/chiller and every tunable including
    TIME_SCALE are the operator's and survive a reset.

    Setting `a_thermal_prev` makes the rebase at main.py:361 a no-op on this tick, so a
    reset that arrives in the same message as an A_THERMAL write lands on the written
    value and not on a rebase of it. `apply_updates` already logs the write at INFO, so
    nothing is logged here.

    +    "reset": "RESET",     — main.py:58, in _ENV_FOR

    Not optional: `cmd` is built at import from every `direction: input` descriptor and
    indexes `_ENV_FOR[name]` (main.py:80-83). Without the entry the new signal raises
    KeyError at import and the service never starts.

(9) main.py:105 and main.py:335 — the initial pack temperature, not the initial heat

    +    INITIAL_TEMPERATURE_C = 20.0

    -    heat = 100_000.0
         with state_lock:
             a_thermal_prev = params["A_THERMAL"]
    +    heat = INITIAL_TEMPERATURE_C / a_thermal_prev
         temperature = a_thermal_prev * heat

    The startup temperature was A_THERMAL x 100 000 J, i.e. a function of the thermal
    mass: 20 °C at the stock 0,0002, but 100 °C at 0,001 — clamped on the first tick to
    MAX_BATTERY_TEMP, so the service would boot at its ceiling. Deriving `heat` from the
    temperature inverts the dependency: the model starts at 20 °C whatever A_THERMAL is,
    and retuning A_THERMAL changes only how fast it moves. The `heat_j` descriptor's
    `default` of 100 000 is now the value at the stock A_THERMAL only; it is an output
    descriptor, read by nothing in main.py (SIGNAL_SPEC keeps `direction: input` only).

    The four trace sha256s hold, because plant/loader.py moved onto the constant in the
    same change. It used to prime each scenario's initial pack temperature by doctoring
    A_THERMAL to t_batt_c / 100 000 and restoring it inside get_producer(), which left
    the plant to rebase `heat` on tick 0; it now sets module.INITIAL_TEMPERATURE_C =
    t_batt_c and leaves A_THERMAL at the scenario's real thermal mass, so no rebase
    fires and tick 0 opens on the same two doubles as before — T1 35 °C / 175 000 J,
    T2 and T3 25 °C / 125 000 J, T4 20 °C / 100 000 J, each a bit-exact round trip at
    the stock A_THERMAL of 0,0002. Had loader.py not moved, all four traces would have
    started at 20 °C and T1's expected FAIL, not just its digest, would have been at
    risk. loader.py is host code and not vendored — see the file list at the top — so
    it is named here only as the reason this edit is hash-safe.


